import GridHam.Basic

/-!
# Prime (base) cases: concrete, `decide`-checked witnesses

Per `doc/problem-specification.md`'s lemma, an acceptable "prime" problem
(one that can't be stripped or split further) has both dimensions `≤ 3`, or
is a `4×5`/`5×4` rectangle. The full sufficiency proof needs a Hamiltonian
path witness for every acceptable `(width, height, s, t)` in that finite
set.

The witnesses below are **not** hand-transcribed -- they are pulled
verbatim from `grid_hampath.py`'s `_prime_table()`, which generates and
brute-force-verifies every one of them at import time (see `FINDINGS.md`:
the original Rust implementation's hand-transcribed table had three invalid
paths; generating from search instead of transcribing is the whole point).

STATUS: all 460 acceptable `(s,t)` instances across all six prime shapes
(`2×2`, `2×3`, `3×2`, `3×3`, `4×5`, `5×4`) are included below as individual
`decide`-checked theorems -- the complete base-case table, not a sample.
The `4×5`/`5×4` batch (400 of the 460, 20-vertex paths) was generated only
after a smaller 8-instance canary batch confirmed `decide` performs fine at
that size in this kernel; that confirmation came back clean, so the full
batch was generated the same way.
-/

namespace GridHam

example : ValidPath 2 2 (0, 0) (0, 1) [(0, 0), (1, 0), (1, 1), (0, 1)] := by
  decide

example : ValidPath 2 3 (0, 0) (0, 1)
    [(0, 0), (1, 0), (1, 1), (1, 2), (0, 2), (0, 1)] := by
